Keywords: **coverage**, **debugging**, **defect**, **error**, **failure**, **quality**, **quality assurance**, **root cause**, **test analysis**, **test basis**, **test case**, **test completion**, **test condition**, **test control**, **test data**, **test design**, **test execution**, **test implementation**, **test monitoring**, **test object**, **test objective**, **test planning**, **test procedure**, **test process**, **test result**, **testing**, **testware**, **traceability**, **validation**, **verification**.

What is Software **Testing**?
Software **testing** is a set of activities across the software development lifecycle (**SDLC**) that helps discover **defects** and evaluate the **quality** of work products (artifacts such as requirements, user stories, designs, code, configurations, and **testware**).

Common Misconception vs. Reality:
• Misconception: **Testing** consists only of executing the software (running tests) and comparing actual vs. expected results.
• Reality: **Test execution** is only one part of **testing**. **Testing** also covers **test planning**, **test monitoring** and **test control**, **test analysis**, **test design**, **test implementation**, and **test completion**, as well as evaluating work products before or without running the code.

**Test Object**, **Testware**, and Expected Results:
• **Test object**: The work product being tested (e.g., a component, mobile app build, system, or specification/model under review).
• **Testware**: Work products created or used during **testing** (test plans, **test conditions**, **test cases**, **test data**, test scripts, test logs, defect reports, test completion reports).
• Expected results (test oracle): The anticipated behavior or output derived from the **test basis** (requirements, specifications, user stories, standards) against which actual **test results** are evaluated.

Dynamic **Testing** vs. Static **Testing**:
• Dynamic **testing** evaluates a **test object** by executing the software with test inputs and observing actual runtime behavior.
• Static **testing** evaluates work products without executing the code—through reviews and static analysis—finding **defects** directly in artifacts or hard-to-reach logic.
• Both dynamic and static **testing** are essential and complement each other.

**Test Objectives**:
Depending on context, test level, and stakeholder needs, **test objectives** include:
• Evaluating work products (requirements, user stories, designs, code).
• Causing **failures** and finding **defects**.
• Ensuring required **coverage** of a **test object**.
• Reducing the risk level of inadequate software **quality**.
• Providing sufficient information to stakeholders to make informed decisions about **quality** and release readiness.
• Complying with contractual, legal, or regulatory requirements or standards, and verifying compliance.
• Building confidence in the **quality** of the **test object**.
• **Validating** whether the **test object** is complete and works as expected by stakeholders and users.

**Testing** vs. **Debugging**:
• **Testing** identifies **failures** or finds **defects** in work products; **testing** itself does not fix **defects**.
• **Debugging** finds, analyzes, and fixes the **defects** that caused a **failure** (typically through reproduction of the **failure**, diagnosis/finding the **defect**, and fixing the **defect**), followed by confirmation **testing** to verify the fix and potentially regression **testing** to check for unintended side effects.

**Errors**, **Defects**, **Failures**, and **Root Causes**:
• **Error** (mistake): A human action that produces an incorrect result.
• **Defect** (fault/bug): An imperfection or deficiency in a work product where it does not meet its requirements or specifications. A **defect** can remain dormant and not cause a **failure** unless specific inputs, states, or conditions trigger it during execution.
• **Failure**: An event in which a component or system does not perform a required function within specified limits. Most **failures** are triggered by **defects** under certain conditions, though external environmental or infrastructure conditions can also cause **failures**.
• **Root cause**: A fundamental underlying reason for a problem that helps prevent similar **defects** or **failures** in the future.

**Verification** vs. **Validation**:
• **Verification** checks whether a work product or system meets specified requirements ("Are we building the product right?", e.g., checking that a specification or API response matches documented rules).
• **Validation** checks whether the system meets users' and stakeholders' needs in its operational environment ("Are we building the right product?", e.g., confirming that real users can complete their operational tasks effectively).
