            title = "5.1 Iteration & Release Planning, Test Case Prioritization",
            theory = """
Keywords: **release planning**, **iteration planning**, **test case prioritization**, **risk-based prioritization**, **coverage-based prioritization**, **requirements-based prioritization**, **test execution schedule**.

1. Tester's Contribution to Release Planning (FL-5.1.2):
- In iterative SDLCs two kinds of planning typically occur: **release planning** and **iteration planning**.
- **Release planning** looks ahead to the release of the product, defines and re-defines the product backlog, and may refine large user stories into smaller ones. It is the basis for the **test approach** and **test plan** across all iterations.
- Testers in release planning: take part in writing **testable user stories** and **acceptance criteria**; take part in project and quality (product) **risk analysis**; **estimate test effort** related to user stories; determine the **test approach**; plan the testing for the release.

2. Tester's Contribution to Iteration Planning (FL-5.1.2):
- **Iteration planning** looks ahead to the end of a single iteration and is concerned with the **iteration backlog**.
- Testers in iteration planning: take part in the **detailed risk analysis** of user stories; determine the **testability** of user stories; **break user stories down into tasks** (particularly testing tasks); **estimate test effort** for all testing tasks; identify and refine functional and non-functional aspects of the test object.
- Exam nuance: release planning = whole release, test approach and plan across iterations; iteration planning = one iteration, task breakdown and detailed estimates.

3. Test Case Prioritization (FL-5.1.5):
- Once test cases and procedures are assembled into test suites, they are arranged in a **test execution schedule** that defines the order of execution.
- **Risk-based prioritization**: order is based on the results of risk analysis; test cases covering the most important risks run first.
- **Coverage-based prioritization**: order is based on coverage (e.g., statement coverage); test cases achieving the highest coverage run first. In the variant **additional coverage prioritization**, the test case with the highest coverage runs first and each next test case is the one that adds the highest additional coverage.
- **Requirements-based prioritization**: order is based on the priorities of the requirements traced to the test cases; requirement priorities are defined by stakeholders; test cases of the most important requirements run first.
- The ideal order may not work when there are **dependencies**: if a higher-priority test case depends on a lower-priority one, the lower-priority test case must run first.
- The order must also respect **resource availability** (tools, environments, people available only in a specific time window).

4. Worked Example (additional coverage):
- Statements 1-9. TC1 covers {1,2,3,4,5,6}; TC2 covers {1,2,3}; TC3 covers {5,6,7,8}; TC4 covers {9}.
- Plain coverage-based order (by total coverage): TC1 (6), TC3 (4), TC2 (3), TC4 (1).
- Additional coverage order: TC1 first (6 statements). Then TC3 adds {7,8} = 2, TC4 adds {9} = 1, TC2 adds 0. Order: TC1, TC3, TC4, TC2.
                        """.trimIndent(),
            practiceQuestionId = 8041,
            language = "EN"
        ),
        StudyModule(
