            source = "SYLLABUS",
            title = "5.1 Test Pyramid & Testing Quadrants",
            theory = """
Keywords: **test pyramid**, **testing quadrants**, **test granularity**, **test isolation**, **test automation**, **business facing**, **technology facing**.

1. The Test Pyramid (FL-5.1.6):
- The **test pyramid** is a model showing that different tests have different **granularity**. It supports the team in **test automation** and in **test effort allocation** by showing that different test objectives are supported by different levels of test automation.
- The layers represent groups of tests. The **higher** the layer, the **lower** the test granularity, the **lower** the test isolation (the degree of dependency on other elements of the system) and the **higher** the test execution time.
- **Bottom layer**: small, isolated, fast tests that check a small piece of functionality; usually **many** of them are needed to reach reasonable coverage.
- **Top layer**: complex, high-level, end-to-end tests; slower, they check a large piece of functionality; usually **few** of them are needed.
- The number and naming of layers may differ. The original model (Cohn 2009) has three layers: **unit tests**, **service tests**, **UI tests**. Another popular model: unit (component) tests, integration (component integration) tests and end-to-end tests. Other test levels can also be used.

2. Testing Quadrants (FL-5.1.7):
- The **testing quadrants** (Brian Marick; Crispin) group test levels with appropriate test types, activities, techniques and work products in Agile development. They help test management visualize that all appropriate test types and test levels are included in the SDLC, and help describe test types to all stakeholders (developers, testers, business representatives).
- Two viewpoints: tests are **business facing** or **technology facing**; tests **support the team** (guide development) or **critique the product** (measure behavior against expectations).
- **Q1** (technology facing, support the team): **component tests** and **component integration tests**; should be automated and included in the CI process.
- **Q2** (business facing, support the team): **functional tests, examples, user story tests, user experience prototypes, API testing, simulations**; check acceptance criteria; manual or automated.
- **Q3** (business facing, critique the product): **exploratory testing, usability testing, user acceptance testing**; user-oriented, often manual.
- **Q4** (technology facing, critique the product): **smoke tests** and **non-functional tests** (except usability tests); often automated.
- Exam trap: usability testing belongs to Q3, not Q4; component tests belong to Q1, not Q2.
                        """.trimIndent(),
            practiceQuestionId = 8053,
            language = "EN"
        ),
