            id = 509,
            chapter = 5,
            source = "SYLLABUS",
            title = "5.3 Test Reports & Communicating Test Status",
            theory = """
Keywords: **test reporting**, **test progress report**, **test completion report**, **dashboard**, **burn-down chart**, **audience**.

1. Purpose of Test Reporting (FL-5.3.2):
- **Test reporting** summarizes and communicates test information during and after testing.
- **Test progress reports** support ongoing **test control** and must give enough information to change the test schedule, resources or test plan when needed because of deviations or changed circumstances.
- **Test completion reports** summarize a specific test activity (test level, test cycle, iteration) and can give information for subsequent testing.

2. Typical Content:
- **Test progress report** (generated regularly, e.g., daily or weekly): testing period; test progress (ahead or behind schedule) with notable deviations; impediments for testing and their workarounds; test metrics; new and changed risks within the testing period; testing planned for the next period.
- **Test completion report** (prepared when a project, test level or test type is complete and ideally exit criteria are met; uses progress reports and other data): test summary; testing and product quality evaluation against the original test plan (objectives and exit criteria); deviations from the test plan (schedule, duration, effort); testing impediments and workarounds; test metrics; unmitigated risks and defects not fixed; lessons learned relevant to testing.
- ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 provides templates for test progress reports (called test status reports) and test completion reports.

3. Audience:
- Different audiences need different information and influence the **formality** and **frequency** of reporting.
- Progress reporting inside the same team is often frequent and informal; completion reporting follows a set template and occurs only once.

4. Communicating the Status of Testing (FL-5.3.3):
- Options: **verbal communication** with team members and stakeholders; **dashboards** (CI/CD dashboards, task boards, burn-down charts); **electronic channels** (email, chat); **online documentation**; **formal test reports**. One or more can be used.
- The best means depends on test management concerns, the organization's test strategy, regulatory standards or, for self-organizing teams, the team itself.
- More formal communication may suit **distributed teams** where face-to-face contact is limited by distance or time differences.
- Different stakeholders are interested in different information, so communication should be **tailored**.
                        """.trimIndent(),
            practiceQuestionId = 8062,
            language = "EN"
