            theory = """
Keywords: configuration management tool, defect management tool, deployment tool, monitoring tool, non-functional testing tool, static testing tool, test design tool, test execution tool, test management tool, test tool.

1. **Categories of Test Tools** (ISTQB CTFL v4.0.1):
- **Test tools** support test activities across the lifecycle and reduce manual overhead, improve consistency, and provide objective data:
  - **Test management tools**: support test planning, scheduling, testware organization, progress/coverage tracking, reporting, and traceability;
  - **Static testing tools**: support reviews (e.g., review management/tracking) and **static analysis** (linters, code quality/security scanners);
  - **Test design and test implementation tools**: assist in generating test cases, test data, test scripts, or test environments/virtualization;
  - **Test execution and test coverage tools**: execute automated tests/test harnesses and measure structural or requirement coverage (such as statement/branch coverage);
  - **Non-functional testing tools**: support performance/load/stress testing, security scanning, accessibility, or reliability/usability checks;
  - **DevOps, CI/CD, and operational tools**: support continuous integration/delivery pipelines, build/deployment automation, configuration management, defect tracking, and system monitoring/observability.

2. **Selecting and Introducing a Tool into an Organization**:
- Selecting a tool requires matching organizational needs, constraints, existing architecture/workflows, and team skills—not chasing hype.
- Typical steps when introducing a tool:
  - Evaluate requirements, budget/licensing costs, integration fit, and team skills;
  - Run a **pilot project** (proof of concept / trial on representative scenarios) to assess real usability, maintainability, and return/effort;
  - Provide **training**, establish clear team conventions/guidelines, support ongoing maintenance, and review effectiveness after rollout.
- **Exam nuance**: A tool is an enabler—without proper process alignment, training, and maintenance, even a powerful tool can become unused shelfware or create extra overhead.
